{% doc %}
  A tint laid over background media.

  ## Why this is a snippet and not three lines in each section

  An overlay is the difference between white text that reads and white text that
  disappears over the light half of a photograph. Every section with background
  media needs one, and every one of them needs the same three things right: it
  must cover the media and nothing else, it must sit under the content, and it
  must never intercept a click meant for the button beneath it. Written inline
  that is three chances per section to get one of them wrong.

  ## Solid or gradient

  A solid tint darkens everything equally, which is right when text is centred
  over the whole image. A gradient darkens one edge and leaves the rest alone,
  which is right when text sits at the bottom and the top of the image is the
  part worth showing — the usual case for a hero.

  The gradient comes from Shopify's `color_background` setting, so the merchant
  edits it with the real gradient picker rather than typing CSS. That value is
  already a complete `linear-gradient(...)`, so it goes straight into
  `background` with no parsing.

  ## Opacity is on the element, not baked into the colour

  `rgba()` mixed in Liquid would need the colour split into channels and
  recombined, and it makes the gradient case impossible — a gradient has many
  colours and one opacity. Setting `opacity` on the overlay element covers both
  with one mechanism, and it animates if a section ever wants it to.

  Parameters:
    style     (string)   solid | gradient. Default solid.
    color     (color)    For `solid`.
    gradient  (string)   A `color_background` value, for `gradient`.
    opacity   (number)   0–100. Default 40.
    classes   (string)   Extra classes.

  @example
    {% render 'overlay',
       style: block.settings.overlay_style,
       color: block.settings.overlay_color,
       gradient: block.settings.overlay_gradient,
       opacity: block.settings.overlay_opacity %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign overlay_style = style | default: 'solid'

  # Clamped and coerced, so a stored value outside the current schema range
  # cannot emit `opacity: 140` — which is not invalid CSS, merely ignored, and
  # then the overlay is silently missing.
  assign overlay_opacity = opacity | default: 40 | plus: 0
  if overlay_opacity < 0
    assign overlay_opacity = 0
  endif
  if overlay_opacity > 100
    assign overlay_opacity = 100
  endif
  assign alpha = overlay_opacity | divided_by: 100.0

  if overlay_style == 'gradient'
    assign paint = gradient | default: ''
  else
    assign paint = color | default: ''
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Nothing to paint means nothing to render. An empty `<div>` here would still
  create a stacking context over the media for no reason.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if paint != '' and alpha > 0 -%}
  <div
    class="background-overlay {{ classes }}"
    style="--overlay-paint: {{ paint }}; --overlay-opacity: {{ alpha }};"
    aria-hidden="true"
  ></div>
{%- endif -%}
